{"key": "38634", "prompt": "You transcribe images of JEE exam questions into markdown. The\ntranscription is stored as the question's text and must stand in for the\nimage: a reader of the rendered markdown should see what the printed\nquestion says, and nothing else.\n\nRules:\n\n1. Copy exactly what is printed, typos included. Do not solve, correct,\n   complete or add anything. Reproduce typos and inconsistencies exactly\n   as printed, even if they look like mistakes: if a series printed as\n   1^3 + 2^3 + ... + n^2 looks wrong, still write n^2.\n2. Approximate the layout in markdown: paragraphs and line breaks, lists\n   and tables. Every labelled line (options, statements, numbered or\n   lettered entries, including roman numerals) is a markdown list item\n   starting with \"- \", with the label as printed, e.g. \"- (A) ...\",\n   \"- (iii) ...\", \"- (IV) ...\", \"- STATEMENT 1: ...\". Never write\n   labelled lines as consecutive plain lines. Options printed in a grid\n   go one per line, in label order.\n3. Maths in LaTeX, $...$ inline and $$...$$ display. Chemistry as\n   $\\ce{...}$ (MathJax mhchem). No other delimiters: no \\( \\), no \\[ \\].\n   Use $$...$$ only for an expression printed on its own line; words\n   printed on the same line as an expression stay outside the maths.\n4. Each figure is a fenced code block with the info string diagram, placed\n   where the figure appears. It holds a factual description of what is\n   drawn: shapes, labels, values, arrows and how the parts are arranged,\n   with printed labels and values copied exactly. This block is the only\n   place for anything you infer: only printed text goes outside diagram\n   blocks, and names or identifications you work out (a compound's name,\n   what a circuit is) go inside the block.\n5. No image placeholders, and no text outside the transcription: no\n   introduction, notes, explanation, answer or solution.\n\nNotation, so that every transcription is written the same way:\n\n- Element symbols, formulas, ions, equations and electron configurations\n  in $\\ce{...}$, e.g. $\\ce{H2SO4}$, $\\ce{Fe^{3+}}$,\n  $\\ce{2H2 + O2 -> 2H2O}$, $\\ce{[Kr] 4d^{10} 5s^1}$.\n- Function names as \\sin, \\cos, \\tan, \\ln, \\log, and\n  \\operatorname{cosec} for cosec.\n- Printed words go outside maths as plain text. Do not wrap prose in\n  \\text{...} inside $...$. Single letters used as labels or variables in\n  an expression are maths: $a > b > c > d$.\n- Units as printed, set upright after the number: $10\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$\n  if the page prints m/s², $10\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$ if it prints m s⁻².\n- Degrees as ^\\circ, e.g. $30^\\circ$.\n- No \\left and \\right unless the printed brackets are visibly sized to\n  their contents.\n- A blank to fill in is written \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ (escaped underscores).\n- Leave out the question number printed before the question, any heading\n  that only names question numbers (such as \"Paragraph for Questions 45\n  and 46\"), and any page furniture (headers, footers, watermarks, marks\n  for the question).\n\nTables (match lists, data tables):\n\n- One table column per printed column. A label and its text go in the\n  same cell, e.g. \"A. 2-Methylpropene and but-1-ene\".\n- All printed title lines of a column go in its header cell, e.g.\n  \"List-I: Pair of Compounds\". Use no empty columns or rows of your own.\n- If columns have different lengths, leave the missing cells empty.\n- Inside a table, write a vertical bar in maths as \\lvert and \\rvert,\n  never as |, which would break the table.\n\nA two-column match list printed with the titles \"Column I\" / \"Process\"\nand \"Column II\" / \"Sign of ΔS\" is written:\n\n| Column I: Process | Column II: Sign of ΔS |\n|---|---|\n| (A) Melting of ice | (p) Positive |\n| (B) Freezing of water | (q) Negative |\n| | (r) Zero |\n\nDiagram blocks:\n\n- Plain text only inside a diagram block: no $, no LaTeX. Use Unicode\n  symbols (°, θ, ², ⁻, →).\n- Each drawn chemical structure gets its own diagram block, with its\n  printed label if it has one, and a last line `smiles: <SMILES>`.\n- Arrows, reagents and conditions printed between structures are printed\n  text: write them outside the blocks, e.g. $\\ce{->[AlCl3]}$ P.\n- If an option or a table cell is a figure, write only its label there,\n  and put the diagram block after the list or table, starting its\n  description with that label, e.g. \"Option (B): ...\" or\n  \"Column I (C): ...\".\n\nA diagram block looks like this:\n\n```diagram\nA block of mass m on a smooth incline at angle θ to the horizontal,\nattached by a string over a pulley at the top to a hanging mass M.\n```\n\nA structure looks like this:\n\n```diagram\nStructure (II): a cyclohexane ring carrying a COOH group.\nsmiles: OC(=O)C1CCCCC1\n```\n\nReply with the transcription only, as plain markdown, not wrapped in a\ncode fence.\n\nThis question:\n\n- Subject: Physics\n- Type: Linked comprehension\n\nLinked comprehension. A passage is shared by several questions; each\nquestion is transcribed with its options as a list, if there are any.\n\nThe image is one question of the passage below. The passage is already\ntranscribed; it is given only so you can understand the question. Do not\ntranscribe it again or repeat any of it: transcribe only the question in\nthe image, with its options.\n\nPassage:\n\nAn experiment is performed on thin lens in order to determine the radii $R_1$ and $R_2$ of the curvature of its two surfaces and the refractive index '$\\mu$' of its glass.\n\n**Experiment I:** A real object is placed $36\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ to the left of lens and a real image is formed $72\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ to the right of lens.\n\n**Experiment II:** The lens is floated on the surface of mercury with surface having radius of curvature $R_2$ faces air. A real object is placed at $60\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ above it, and a real image is formed at $30\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ above it (assume mercury to be perfectly reflecting).\n\n**Experiment III:** 